WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.931
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level one.

2
00:00:06.231 --> 00:00:09.710
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:16.041
Unit one: Reading Local News. Today you will read and write news headlines in the present simple.

4
00:00:16.341 --> 00:00:18.714
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:22.656
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.517 --> 00:00:24.378
Headline.

7
00:00:25.383 --> 00:00:28.439
Headline means the short title at the top of a news story.

8
00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:31.282
The headline caught everyone's attention.

9
00:00:31.883 --> 00:00:34.721
Your turn. Say: Headline.

10
00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:38.475
Headline.

11
00:00:39.217 --> 00:00:40.183
Reporter.

12
00:00:41.183 --> 00:00:44.483
Reporter means a person who gathers and writes news stories.

13
00:00:45.083 --> 00:00:46.851
The reporter interviewed the mayor.

14
00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:50.009
Your turn. Say: Reporter.

15
00:00:52.867 --> 00:00:53.798
Reporter.

16
00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:55.330
Byline.

17
00:00:56.317 --> 00:00:59.315
Byline means the line that tells you who wrote the article.

18
00:00:59.917 --> 00:01:01.893
The byline shows the writer's name.

19
00:01:02.483 --> 00:01:04.857
Your turn. Say: Byline.

20
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:08.608
Byline.

21
00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:10.374
Source.

22
00:01:11.383 --> 00:01:15.682
Source means a person or document that gives information for a story.

23
00:01:16.283 --> 00:01:18.585
A good reporter always checks the source.

24
00:01:19.183 --> 00:01:21.627
Your turn. Say: Source.

25
00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:25.294
Source.

26
00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:27.202
Breaking news.

27
00:01:28.217 --> 00:01:32.202
Breaking news means news that has just happened and is being reported at once.

28
00:01:32.817 --> 00:01:34.874
Breaking news: a bridge has reopened.

29
00:01:35.483 --> 00:01:38.101
Your turn. Say: Breaking news.

30
00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:42.200
Breaking news.

31
00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:43.881
Update.

32
00:01:44.883 --> 00:01:47.533
Update means new information added to a story.

33
00:01:48.117 --> 00:01:50.337
The paper published an update at noon.

34
00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:53.312
Your turn. Say: Update.

35
00:01:56.167 --> 00:01:57.017
Update.

36
00:01:57.783 --> 00:01:58.726
Local.

37
00:01:59.717 --> 00:02:02.668
Local means connected with a particular town or area.

38
00:02:03.283 --> 00:02:05.887
Local news tells us what is happening near us.

39
00:02:06.483 --> 00:02:08.729
Your turn. Say: Local.

40
00:02:11.567 --> 00:02:12.417
Local.

41
00:02:13.183 --> 00:02:14.103
Community.

42
00:02:15.117 --> 00:02:18.718
Community means a group of people who live in the same area.

43
00:02:19.317 --> 00:02:21.548
The whole community came to the festival.

44
00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:24.651
Your turn. Say: Community.

45
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:28.478
Community.

46
00:02:29.267 --> 00:02:32.720
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:33.583 --> 00:02:40.755
Visal, look at this headline: 'Mayor opens new market'. It is about last week, but the verb is in the present. Why?

48
00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:50.249
Headlines use the present simple for past events. It makes the news feel fresh and keeps the headline short.

49
00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:53.384
And what do I find under the headline?

50
00:02:54.483 --> 00:03:01.722
The byline names the reporter. The article then gives the full story in the past tense, with each source named.

51
00:03:02.817 --> 00:03:05.850
So a local story can matter to the whole community?

52
00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:14.746
Yes. Local news is close to our daily lives, and a breaking news update can help people act quickly.

53
00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:17.760
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:18.617 --> 00:03:21.673
Grammar focus: Present simple in headlines.

55
00:03:21.973 --> 00:03:23.125
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:23.817 --> 00:03:25.468
Mayor opens new market.

57
00:03:26.383 --> 00:03:28.058
Flood closes main road.

58
00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:31.042
Students win national prize.

59
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:38.994
Headlines use the present simple for events that already happened. Drop small words like 'the' and 'a': 'Mayor opens market'.

60
00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:43.656
Be careful! Don't say: Mayor opened new market (headline)

61
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.971
Say: Mayor opens new market

62
00:03:48.271 --> 00:03:51.083
Headlines use the present simple for past events.

63
00:03:51.867 --> 00:03:54.554
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:55.367 --> 00:03:57.146
Why Local News Matters.

65
00:03:57.446 --> 00:04:01.941
A local newspaper tells a community what is happening on its own streets.

66
00:04:02.933 --> 00:04:07.998
A reporter gathers facts from a reliable source and writes a clear headline above the story.

67
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.449
The byline shows who wrote the article, so readers know who is responsible for it.

68
00:04:14.433 --> 00:04:19.010
When breaking news arrives, the paper adds an update as soon as new facts are confirmed.

69
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.996
In this way, local news helps neighbours stay informed and work together.

70
00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:28.986
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:32.720
Headlines use the present simple for blank events.

72
00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:36.616
A, past. B, future. C, imaginary.

73
00:04:40.167 --> 00:04:42.062
The answer is A: past.

74
00:04:43.217 --> 00:04:45.448
The line that names the writer is the blank.

75
00:04:45.748 --> 00:04:49.100
A, headline. B, byline. C, source.

76
00:04:52.633 --> 00:04:54.714
The answer is B: byline.

77
00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:58.535
News that has just happened is blank news.

78
00:04:58.835 --> 00:05:01.733
A, old. B, breaking. C, local.

79
00:05:05.267 --> 00:05:07.289
The answer is B: breaking.

80
00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:09.962
Choose the best headline.

81
00:05:10.262 --> 00:05:15.436
A, Mayor opened the new market. B, Mayor opens new market. C, Mayor is open market.

82
00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:21.952
The answer is B: Mayor opens new market.

83
00:05:23.117 --> 00:05:25.859
According to the reading, a paper adds an update when…

84
00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:31.426
A, a reader complains. B, new facts are confirmed. C, it has no source.

85
00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:37.721
The answer is B: new facts are confirmed.

86
00:05:38.933 --> 00:05:42.282
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:43.117 --> 00:05:46.173
What makes a local news story important to a community?

88
00:05:54.983 --> 00:05:56.495
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:56.795 --> 00:06:19.875
A local story is important because it affects people's daily lives. For example, news about a closed road or a new clinic tells neighbours what to do next. It also builds trust, because reporters live in the same community. Finally, local news gives ordinary people a voice and helps them solve problems together.

90
00:06:20.175 --> 00:06:21.989
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:22.883 --> 00:06:27.649
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and present simple in headlines.

92
00:06:27.949 --> 00:06:34.316
Today's value is community awareness. Notice how changes affect every neighbour, not only the winners.

93
00:06:34.616 --> 00:06:36.978
See you in Unit two. Goodbye!

94
00:06:37.278 --> 00:06:40.441
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

